 "evidence": "The paper describes that C. difficile initiates a sporulation pathway triggered by environmental stimuli such as nutrient starvation, quorum sensing, and other stress factors, producing dormant spores that are resistant to heat, oxygen, and disinfectants. Germination occurs in the lower gastrointestinal tract, induced by bile acids like taurocholate, while chenodeoxycholate inhibits germination."
 },
 {
 "mechanism": "Inhibition of spore formation by antimicrobial agents",
 "evidence": "Fidaxomicin inhibits sporulation and outgrowth of vegetative cells from germinating spores at sub-MICs, while vancomycin and metronidazole do not. Cadazolid and ridinilazole also inhibit spore formation in vitro."
 },
 {
 "mechanism": "Prolonged adherence to exosporium as a spore inhibition strategy",
 "evidence": "Ramoplanin adheres to the exosporium for a prolonged period, allowing it to attack germinating cells. Vancomycin-loaded iron oxide nanoparticles selectively bind to C. difficile spores and delay germination."
 },
 {
 "mechanism": "Gut microbiota disruption and CDI recurrence",
 "evidence": "CDI recurrence is multifactorial, with gut microbiota disruption coincident with viable C. difficile spores considered the most important factor. Fidaxomicin preserves microbiota diversity better than vancomycin, contributing to lower recurrence rates."
 }
